**Pull-based execution — the terminal drives:**

```java
// This builds a PIPELINE DESCRIPTION — nothing executes:
Stream<Trade> pipeline = trades.stream()    // source descriptor
    .filter(Trade::isSettled)                // adds filter stage
    .map(Trade::getAmount);                  // adds map stage

// NOW the terminal triggers pull-based execution:
Optional<BigDecimal> first = pipeline.findFirst();

// Execution (pull-based, element by element):
// findFirst says: "give me ONE element"
//   → asks map: "give me ONE element"
//     → asks filter: "give me ONE element"
//       → asks source: "give me ONE element"
//         → source yields Trade TRD-001
//       → filter checks: isSettled? YES → passes to map
//     → map transforms: Trade → BigDecimal (245050)
//   → findFirst receives 245050 → DONE! Pipeline stops.
// TRD-002, TRD-003, etc. are NEVER pulled from the source!
```

**One element in-flight — NOT buffered (unless stateful):**

```java
// STATELESS ops (filter, map, flatMap): one element at a time, O(1) memory
trades.stream()
    .filter(Trade::isSettled)   // checks ONE trade, passes or drops
    .map(Trade::getAmount)      // transforms ONE trade, passes result
    .forEach(System.out::println);
// At any instant, only 1 Trade is being processed.
// 10 million trades? Still O(1) memory for filter+map!

// STATEFUL ops (sorted, distinct): MUST buffer ALL elements
trades.stream()
    .sorted(Comparator.comparing(Trade::getAmount))  // BUFFERS entire stream!
    .limit(5)
    .toList();
// sorted() must see ALL elements before it can sort → O(n) memory
// distinct() must track seen elements in a HashSet → O(n) memory
```

**Fusion — multiple ops execute in ONE pass:**

```java
// This does NOT make 3 separate passes over the data:
trades.stream()
    .filter(Trade::isSettled)    // pass 1? NO!
    .map(Trade::getAmount)       // pass 2? NO!
    .forEach(System.out::println); // pass 3? NO!

// ONE PASS: for each element pulled from source:
//   1. filter checks isSettled
//   2. if passes → map transforms to amount
//   3. forEach prints
// All 3 operations happen on SAME element before moving to next!
// This is called LOOP FUSION — the JVM fuses the ops into a single loop.
```

**Infinite streams — only possible because of laziness + pull-based:**

```java
// This would be IMPOSSIBLE if stream stored elements:
Stream.generate(() -> Math.random())     // infinite source!
    .filter(n -> n > 0.9)                // filter (lazy)
    .limit(5)                            // short-circuit: stop after 5
    .toList();                           // terminal → triggers pull
// Pulls random numbers one at a time.
// When filter passes 5 numbers, limit says "enough!" → pipeline stops.
// Never tries to "store" infinite numbers in memory.
```

